WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.640
Hello everyone! You are watching&nbsp;
a Business English series brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.800
to you by British Council English&nbsp;
Online. We’re hoping to equip you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:15.840
with the practical skills you need to thrive in&nbsp;the 
international, English-speaking workspace.&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.400
Today is episode 2 of the conflict&nbsp;
management series. I’m going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.320
give you practical tips on how to manage
conflict effectively in the workspace.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.840
Click on the little 'i' up here and it&nbsp;
will take you to the first episode.&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:34.960
Alright, we’re going to go&nbsp;
straight in with tip number 3.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:42.800
Be specific about issue and impact.
When entering into a conversation that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.680
attempting to manage conflict,&nbsp;
saying things like 'you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:55.520
always' or 'you never' is never a good idea. Sweeping
statements like these will often result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.560
in a defensive response. And to be honest, I&nbsp;can understand
why. If someone said to you,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.760
'you always complain' you’d probably think,
'well actually I can think of quite a few

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.800
examples and situations where I didn’t.'
You always criticise what I do!&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.920
Oh hey! Your presentation&nbsp;
was really well executed.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.200
Yeah that’s really well written.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:28.080
Be objective and avoid personal attacks.&nbsp;
So, for example, instead of saying&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.120
'You always criticize what I do'
 You could say:

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:36.160
'I didn’t appreciate it when you criticized&nbsp;my work
in front of our partners yesterday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:43.360
in the meeting at 10am, specifically, 10:32.'
Here you’re pinpointing the pain point.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.560
Pinpoint the pain point. 
Apparently, you remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.200
things more when it becomes a melody. 
Remember, be specific about an issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:59.280
and avoid, stay away from generalisations.  
Number 4: Don’t bring up past events.&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.520
Anybody who has ever been to marriage&nbsp;
counselling, couple counselling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.080
friend counselling, any kind of counselling&nbsp;
will have heard this advice before. &nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:14.480
We often bring up past events as a kind&nbsp;
of trump card hoping it will put us in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:20.640
position of power within that conversation. 
I just wanted to say that it felt a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.280
bit condescending yesterday when&nbsp;
you spoke over me in the meeting.&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:31.120
Okay but, you spoke over me in the board meeting&nbsp;last
year, so now you know what it feels like.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:37.040
Just because our friend here remembered how their
colleague acted one year ago, doesn’t resolve the

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.360
fact that they interrupted them yesterday.   
Here’s what we need to do. Take that list from

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.160
out of your back pocket, the one where you’ve&nbsp;noted
all the things that you didn’t like about

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.360
your colleague over the past 2 years. Take&nbsp;that
list, and tear it up, blow it away.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.520
When you bring up past events, you’re&nbsp;
distracting people from the main point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:01.520
of the conversation, which doesn’t resolve why
you’re in a conflict in the first place.&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.400
See you in episode three for our last 2 tips.
Bye!

